          <caption>
            <p>Buphthalmos with Bilateral Tearing and Light Sensitivity. The image shows buphthalmos in a 9-month-old boy who presents with bilateral tearing and light sensitivity. Bilateral swelling&#x000a0;of the globes is&#x000a0;indicated&#x000a0;by the enlargement of the corneas, with tears in the Descemet membrane and inferior corneal edema. The sclera shows a bluish appearance&#x000a0;due to its thinning&#x000a0;and&#x000a0;the uveal tissue showing through. The corneal diameter is 13 mm (normal is 10.5 mm in a child this age). The intraocular pressures&#x000a0;are 35 mm Hg in the right and 38 mm Hg in the left. Contributed by BCK Patel, MD, FRCS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Buphthalmos-bck__patel" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
